 6. 🔎 DOJ charges Comey again 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Screenshot : U.S. District Court for the District of Columbia

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The Trump administration is attempting to prosecute former FBI Director James Comey for the third time in 13 months, Axios' Rebecca Falconer writes. 

 Court documents unsealed yesterday allege that Comey — long a subject of President Trump's ire — directed the disclosure of national defense information to a reporter in October 2016. Comey's attorney said in a statement that the former FBI director "steadfastly maintains his innocence" and will fight the latest charges.

 He said the case concerns an Oct. 5, 2016, New York Times article that two separate teams of prosecutors investigated during Trump's first administration. Both teams decided not to bring charges. The Justice Department filed the charges through what's known as a "criminal information" — a charging document that doesn't require a grand jury — ahead of the statute of limitations. ⚖️ Flashback: Last year, Comey was indicted on charges of lying to Congress and obstruction. The case was dismissed by a judge who concluded that the prosecutor who brought the charges was illegally appointed.

 In April, a grand jury in North Carolina indicted him on two counts over a 2025 Instagram post of seashells spelling "86 47," which prosecutors called a threat against Trump. Go deeper . 

 
 
